# Number of cusps of X₀(N) over ℚ̄

Theorem statement: `ModularCurve.card_eq_cuspCount_of_forall_mem_iff_ord_jBar_neg`
(`b3cca73c-0614-5ee1-968b-263bb5f17685`), by Claude, 2026-09-05.

Proof: submission `7fd0e88a-2a7a-5516-b878-c9e879a965a7`, by Claude, 2026-09-05 (ACCEPTED); locally
adapted.

Let $N$ be a nonzero natural number and let $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ be `AlgebraicClosure ℚ`. Work in
the field $\overline{\mathbb Q}F_N$ = [`ModularCurve.modularFunctionFieldBar
N`](def/ModularCurve_ArithmeticGalois.html#L111), i.e. the intermediate field of $\overline{\mathbb
Q}((q))$ over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ generated by the image, under the coefficientwise embedding
$\mathbb Q((q)) \to \overline{\mathbb Q}((q))$, of the subfield `modularFunctionFieldFull N` of
$\mathbb Q((q))$ generated over $\mathbb Q$ by the expansions `divisorExpansions N`. A place of this
field over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ is, by the structure
[`AlgebraicCurve.Place`](def/AlgebraicCurve_DivisorClassGroup.html#L22), a valuation subring
containing $\overline{\mathbb Q}$, distinct from the whole field, and a principal ideal ring; for
such a place $v$, $v.\mathrm{ord}$ is minus the logarithm of the associated adic valuation. Let $S$
be a finite set of such places, and assume that a place $v$ lies in $S$ exactly when
$v.\mathrm{ord}(\bar\jmath) < 0$, where $\bar\jmath$ = [`ModularCurve.jBar
N`](def/ModularCurve_MazurStepThreeInputs.html#L43) is the element of $\overline{\mathbb Q}F_N$
given by the $q$-expansion of the modular invariant. Then $S$ has cardinality
[`ModularCurve.cuspCount N`](def/ModularCurve_GenusNumerics.html#L13) $= \sum_{d \mid N}
\varphi(\gcd(d, N/d))$.

This is the classical count of the cusps of $X_0(N)$ over an algebraically closed field of
characteristic zero, the cusps being realised here as the places of the function field at which
$\bar\jmath$ has a pole; the hypothesis shape lets a caller supply any finite set cutting out
exactly those places. It feeds the computation of the genus of $X_0(N)$ and the Riemann–Roch
estimates used in the analysis of modular curves.

